WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:03.327
<v Examiner>Exam case 14, continued.

2
00:00:03.327 --> 00:00:04.525
<v Examiner>Simon's family.

3
00:00:04.975 --> 00:00:05.630
<v Examiner>Seven marks.

4
00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:09.998
<v Examiner>Draw a pedigree chart showing Simon, his mum, his dad, his maternal grandmother,

5
00:00:09.998 --> 00:00:14.912
<v Examiner>who is not colour blind, his maternal grandfather, who is colour blind, his maternal aunt,

6
00:00:14.912 --> 00:00:17.969
<v Examiner>who is colour blind, and his maternal uncle, who is not.

7
00:00:17.969 --> 00:00:22.447
<v Examiner>Can you track how Simon inherited it, when neither of his parents is colour blind?

8
00:00:22.897 --> 00:00:25.978
Write each genotype on the X chromosome.

9
00:00:25.978 --> 00:00:27.904
X big B is normal vision.

10
00:00:27.904 --> 00:00:30.985
X little b carries the colour blindness.

11
00:00:30.985 --> 00:00:32.833
A man has one X and a Y.

12
00:00:32.833 --> 00:00:34.374
A woman has two X's.

13
00:00:34.824 --> 00:00:39.618
The hook: Grandad's X little b goes to a carrier mum, and then to Simon.

14
00:00:39.618 --> 00:00:41.416
His dad only gives him a Y.

15
00:00:41.866 --> 00:00:44.658
His maternal grandfather: X little b, Y.

16
00:00:44.658 --> 00:00:48.287
A man, colour blind, and the condition is recessive.

17
00:00:48.737 --> 00:00:51.674
His maternal grandmother: X big B, X little b.

18
00:00:51.674 --> 00:00:52.313
A carrier.

19
00:00:52.313 --> 00:00:57.612
She isn't colour blind, but she has a colour-blind daughter, so she must carry one.

20
00:00:58.062 --> 00:01:00.959
His maternal aunt: X little b, X little b.

21
00:01:00.959 --> 00:01:05.443
A colour-blind woman has the defect on both of her X chromosomes.

22
00:01:06.893 --> 00:01:09.234
Simon's mum: X big B, X little b.

23
00:01:09.234 --> 00:01:09.943
A carrier.

24
00:01:09.943 --> 00:01:14.979
She passed it to Simon, but her normal X big B gives her normal vision.

25
00:01:15.429 --> 00:01:17.177
Simon's dad: X big B, Y.

26
00:01:17.177 --> 00:01:20.527
He isn't colour blind, so he has the normal X.

27
00:01:20.977 --> 00:01:23.292
His maternal uncle: X big B, Y.

28
00:01:23.292 --> 00:01:24.561
Not colour blind.

29
00:01:25.011 --> 00:01:26.846
And Simon: X little b, Y.

30
00:01:26.846 --> 00:01:29.268
His X little b came from his mum.

31
00:01:29.268 --> 00:01:31.177
His dad gave him only a Y.

32
00:01:32.627 --> 00:01:33.258
<v Examiner>Six marks.

33
00:01:33.258 --> 00:01:37.043
<v Examiner>Given all that, how is red-green colour blindness inherited?

34
00:01:37.493 --> 00:01:38.663
X-linked recessive.

35
00:01:38.663 --> 00:01:43.099
The genes for the L and M cones, red and green, are on the X chromosome.

36
00:01:43.099 --> 00:01:47.843
It's often carried by mothers, and expressed in fifty per cent of their sons.

37
00:01:47.843 --> 00:01:52.586
It only shows when all the X chromosomes are affected, so a woman needs both.

38
00:01:52.586 --> 00:01:57.207
Fathers never pass it to their sons, because they only give their sons a Y.

39
00:01:57.207 --> 00:02:00.965
Fathers always pass the affected X to all of their daughters.

40
00:02:00.965 --> 00:02:02.751
And it can skip a generation.

41
00:02:03.201 --> 00:02:06.999
That's her "better answer", from her post on what to study for the exam.

42
00:02:08.449 --> 00:02:09.086
<v Examiner>Two marks.

43
00:02:09.086 --> 00:02:13.036
<v Examiner>Will Simon pass red-green colour blindness on to his children?

44
00:02:13.036 --> 00:02:13.291
<v Examiner>How?

45
00:02:13.741 --> 00:02:15.928
To none of his sons: they get his Y.

46
00:02:15.928 --> 00:02:21.214
And to all of his daughters, as unaffected carriers: each daughter gets his X little b.

47
00:02:21.214 --> 00:02:25.771
Her quiz key, word for word: to none of his sons, but to all his daughters,

48
00:02:25.771 --> 00:02:27.715
who will be unaffected carriers.

49
00:02:28.165 --> 00:02:32.405
And her quiz asks for his mum's genotype as a multiple choice.

50
00:02:32.405 --> 00:02:34.594
The answer: X little b, X big B.

51
00:02:34.594 --> 00:02:39.791
She carried it over to Simon, but her normal X big B gave her normal vision.

52
00:02:40.241 --> 00:02:40.907
<v Examiner>Once more.

53
00:02:40.907 --> 00:02:41.905
<v Examiner>Simon's family.

54
00:02:44.355 --> 00:02:47.001
Grandfather, X little b, Y: colour blind.

55
00:02:47.001 --> 00:02:52.487
Grandmother, X big B, X little b: a carrier, because she has a colour-blind daughter.

56
00:02:52.487 --> 00:02:54.359
Aunt, X little b, X little b.

57
00:02:54.359 --> 00:02:55.521
Uncle, X big B, Y.

58
00:02:55.521 --> 00:02:59.135
Mum, X big B, X little b: a carrier, with normal vision.

59
00:02:59.135 --> 00:03:00.167
Dad, X big B, Y.

60
00:03:00.167 --> 00:03:04.685
Simon, X little b, Y: his X came from his mum, and his Y from his dad.

61
00:03:04.685 --> 00:03:09.396
It's X-linked recessive: carried by mothers, shown in half of their sons,

62
00:03:09.396 --> 00:03:14.947
a woman needs both X's, fathers never pass it to sons and always pass it to daughters,

63
00:03:14.947 --> 00:03:16.818
and it can skip a generation.

64
00:03:16.818 --> 00:03:21.207
Simon will pass it to none of his sons, and to all of his daughters,

65
00:03:21.207 --> 00:03:22.691
as unaffected carriers.
